window.docContents[69].resolve({"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/k-linear-abelian-categories/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Remark7___7___4":{"contents":"Let k be a field. We say that an abelian category \\mathcal{C} is k-linear if the groups \\operatorname{Hom}_\\mathcal{C}(X, Y) are equipped with a structure of a vector space over k and composition maps are k-linear in each argument. In particular, the categories in Example 7.7.2 are k-linear.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.7. Abelian categories\tk-linear abelian categories","header":"k-linear abelian categories","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/k-linear-abelian-categories/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Remark7___7___4"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext/#Chapter8___Problem8___2___6":{"contents":"Problem 8.2.6. (i) Show that \\mathrm{Tor}_0(M, N) = M \\otimes_A N and that \\mathrm{Ext}^0(M, N) = \\mathrm{Hom}_A(M, N).\n\n(ii) Show that the group \\mathrm{Ext}^1(M, N) as defined here is canonically isomorphic to the one defined in Problem 3.9.1.\n\n(iii) Let\n\n0 \\to N_1 \\to N_2 \\to N_3 \\to 0\n\nbe a short exact sequence of left A-modules.\n\nShow that there are long exact sequences\n\n0 \\to \\mathrm{Hom}_A(M, N_1) \\to \\mathrm{Hom}_A(M, N_2) \\to \\mathrm{Hom}_A(M, N_3) \\to\n\n\\mathrm{Ext}^1(M, N_1) \\to \\mathrm{Ext}^1(M, N_2) \\to \\mathrm{Ext}^1(M, N_3) \\to \\mathrm{Ext}^2(M, N_1) \\to \\ldots\n\nand\n\n\\ldots \\to \\mathrm{Tor}_2(M, N_3) \\to \\mathrm{Tor}_1(M, N_1) \\to \\mathrm{Tor}_1(M, N_2) \\to \\mathrm{Tor}_1(M, N_3)\n\n\\to M \\otimes_A N_1 \\to M \\otimes_A N_2 \\to M \\otimes_A N_3 \\to 0.\n\nThis shows that, as we mentioned above, the Tor and Ext groups \"quantify\" the extent to which the functors M \\otimes_A and \\mathrm{Hom}_A(M, ?) are not exact.\n\nHint: Use Problem 7.8.5.\n\n(iv) Show that \\mathrm{Tor}_i^A(M, N) can be computed by taking a projective resolution of N, tensoring it with M, and computing cohomology.\nHint: Show first that \\operatorname{Tor}_i^A(M, N) can be computed by tensoring two projective resolutions, for M and for N, and computing cohomology.\n\n(v) Let 0 \\to M_1 \\to M_2 \\to M_3 \\to 0 be a short exact sequence of left A-modules. Let P_\\bullet^1 and P_\\bullet^3 be projective resolutions of M_1 and M_3. Construct a projective resolution of M_2 with terms P_i^2 := P_i^1 \\oplus P_i^3 (use Exercise 8.1.4). Use it to show that for any left A-module N there are long exact sequences\n\n\n0 \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(M_3, N) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(M_2, N) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(M_1, N) \\to\n\n\n\n\\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_3, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_2, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_1, N) \\to \\operatorname{Ext}^2(M_3, N) \\to \\ldots\n\n\nand\n\n\n\\cdots \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_2(M_3, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_1(M_1, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_1(M_2, N) \\to \\operatorname{Tor}_1(M_3, N)\n\n\n\n\\to M_1 \\otimes_A N \\to M_2 \\otimes_A N \\to M_3 \\otimes_A N \\to 0.\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t8. Homological algebra\t8.2. Tor and Ext functors\tProperties of Tor and Ext","header":"Properties of Tor and Ext","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Properties-of-Tor-and-Ext/#Chapter8___Problem8___2___6"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___3-continued___-affine-Dynkin-diagrams-and-parts-_LPAR_f_RPAR_-_LPAR_g_RPAR_/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___3_continued_tildeE":{"contents":"* \\tilde{E}_6:\n\n\\overset{1}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{3}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{1}{\\bullet}\n\n\\hspace{5em} |\n\n\\hspace{5em} \\overset{2}{\\bullet}\n\n\\hspace{5em} |\n\n\\hspace{5em} \\overset{1}{\\bullet}\n\n* \\tilde{E}_7:\n\n\\overset{1}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{3}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{4}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{3}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{1}{\\bullet}\n\n\\hspace{7em} |\n\n\\hspace{7em} \\overset{2}{\\bullet}\n\n* \\tilde{E}_8:\n\n\\overset{1}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{3}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{4}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{5}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{6}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{4}{\\bullet} \\text{---} \\overset{2}{\\bullet}\n\n\\hspace{9em} |\n\n\\hspace{9em} \\overset{3}{\\bullet}\n\nHint for (c)-(e): What is the meaning of the numbers labeling the vertices of these graphs?\n\n(f) Deduce from (a)—(e) the classification theorem for Dynkin diagrams.\n\n(g) A (simply laced) affine Dynkin diagram is a connected graph without self-loops such that the quadratic form defined by A is positive semidefinite but not positive definite. Classify affine Dynkin diagrams. (Show that they are exactly the forbidden diagrams from (c)—(e).)\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.1. Problems\tProblem 6.1.3 continued: affine Dynkin diagrams and parts (f)-(g)","header":"Problem 6.1.3 continued: affine Dynkin diagrams and parts (f)-(g)","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___3-continued___-affine-Dynkin-diagrams-and-parts-_LPAR_f_RPAR_-_LPAR_g_RPAR_/#Chapter6___Problem6___1___3_continued_tildeE"}});
